{"name":"Plate Mail Metal","hex":"#8c8589","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"plate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slightly curved but almost flat dish from which food is served or eaten.","Such dishes collectively."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover the surface material of an object with a thin coat of another material, usually a metal.","To place the various elements of a meal on the diner's plate prior to serving."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plate"},{"term":"mail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bag or wallet. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A bag containing letters to be delivered by post."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To send (a letter, parcel, etc.) through the mail.","To send by electronic mail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mail"},{"term":"metal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Chemical elements or alloys, their ores, and the mines where their ores come from.\n Any of a number of chemical elements in the periodic table that form a metallic bond with other metal atoms; generally shiny, somewhat malleable and hard, often a conductor of heat and electricity.\n\n Any material with similar physical properties as those chemical elements, especially as a combination of several of them, such as an alloy.\n\n An element which was not directly created after the Big Bang but instead formed through nuclear reactions; any element other than hydrogen and helium.\n\n Crushed rock, stones etc. used to make a road.\n\n The ore from which a metal is derived.\n A mine from which ores are taken.","Any of a number of chemical elements in the periodic table that form a metallic bond with other metal atoms; generally shiny, somewhat malleable and hard, often a conductor of heat and electricity."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characterized by strong drum-beats and distorted guitars. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Having the emotional or social characteristics associated with metal music; brash, bold, frank, unyielding, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/metal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plate armour","blocks":[{"text":"Plate armour is a historical type of personal body armour made from bronze, iron, or steel plates, culminating in suits of armour which entirely or mostly encased the wearer. Full plate steel armour developed in Europe during the Late Middle Ages, especially in the context of the Hundred Years' War, from the coat of plates worn over mail suits during the 14th century, a century famous for transitional armour."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Lossy-page1-2122px-H%C3%A4strustning_-_Livrustkammaren_-_72697_aaa.jpg/330px-Lossy-page1-2122px-H%C3%A4strustning_-_Livrustkammaren_-_72697_aaa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plate_armour","wikidata":"Q3391846"},{"title":"Mail","blocks":[{"text":"The mail, or post, is a system for physically transporting postcards, letters, and parcels. A postal service can be private or public, though many governments place restrictions on private systems. Since the mid-19th century, national postal systems have generally been established as a government monopoly, with a fee on the article prepaid. Proof of payment is usually in the form of an adhesive postage stamp, but a postage meter is also used for bulk mailing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/HK_Postman_Collect_Letters.JPG/330px-HK_Postman_Collect_Letters.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mail","wikidata":"Q49845"},{"title":"Metal","blocks":[{"text":"A metal is a material that, when polished or fractured, shows a lustrous appearance, and conducts electricity and heat relatively well. These properties are all associated with having electrons available at the Fermi level, as opposed to nonmetallic materials which do not. Metals are typically ductile and malleable."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal","wikidata":"Q11426"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Rustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Rustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Rustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Rustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Rustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Rustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Armour for Gustav I of Sweden by Kunz Lochner, c. 1540 (Livrustkammaren).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARustning%2C_Gustav_Vasa_-_Livrustkammaren_-_32921.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bronze_cuirass_BM_GR1873.8-20.223.jpg/500px-Bronze_cuirass_BM_GR1873.8-20.223.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bronze_cuirass_BM_GR1873.8-20.223.jpg/1280px-Bronze_cuirass_BM_GR1873.8-20.223.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bronze_cuirass_BM_GR1873.8-20.223.jpg/500px-Bronze_cuirass_BM_GR1873.8-20.223.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia